#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153290} :activity/id #uuid "d59d69ae-798b-5a20-9d5f-60ce55a98799" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522 :topic/id #uuid "503f6e39-70fd-54b8-a04b-77e9fcd7b979" :topic/math-academy-id 2740} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153382 :tutorial/content "Consider the second-order linear homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 5y' + 6y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that a fundamental set of solutions is given by\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle tells us that any linear combination of solutions to a linear, homogeneous differential equation is also a solution to the equation. Therefore, the **general solution** to this differential equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are **arbitrary constants.**\n\nIn general, if an $n$th order homogeneous, linear ODE has a fundamental set of solutions given by \n\n$$\ny_1(x), \\quad y_2(x),\\quad \\ldots, \\quad y_n(x),\n$$\n\nthen the general solution to the equation is \n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = C_1y_1(x)+C_2y_2(x)+\\cdots+C_ny_n(x)}\n$$\n\nfor arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2,\\ldots C_n.$\n\n**Note:** Using sigma notation, we can write the general solution more compactly as\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n C_i y_i(x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d0ae06ba-917f-56b1-8d5d-fac54f464ee8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4197 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c268f62a-7fd0-540c-9ff9-832b5389a31e" :step/math-academy-id 601711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151984 :question/id #uuid "bbf72227-f0da-5dab-88cd-bfed2ed773f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6792"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421821}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "f0a0cdaa-70e3-5041-a39e-64dbcbe7a4a1" :step/math-academy-id 601712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151982 :question/id #uuid "bacb284c-88db-5ad5-bd7d-cbe6fbc604df" :question/math-academy-id "e-6789"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421822}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"} :step/id #uuid "66cf83ce-b3bc-50bd-88ae-2b9f54096657" :step/math-academy-id 601715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153293}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151985 :question/id #uuid "f41bad20-5a72-5d6d-82c4-cf36f7ac74cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6794"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421819}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "38e288bc-7c3f-536d-a984-63e199038d6a" :step/math-academy-id 601718 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153335 :tutorial/content "Similar to first-order linear ODEs, for inhomogeneous differential equations, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the **complementary solution** (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a **particular solution** of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe will apply this structure to a specific differential equation in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "d323d4d9-40c7-5a00-aafe-a0c1ba46fd4c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14780 :tutorial/title "General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "4499c456-0b30-59ea-92cd-0a6e8cc58d01" :step/math-academy-id 601721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151912 :question/id #uuid "58f2bf06-7449-5a66-9662-532ce70a8ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397379}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "2779fb81-53c1-5556-969d-409a83b9a273" :step/math-academy-id 601722}] :activity/title "General Solutions of Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152359}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426295} :activity/id #uuid "d0ccf989-2e1a-5a9a-9ac4-779324bb879f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522 :topic/id #uuid "503f6e39-70fd-54b8-a04b-77e9fcd7b979" :topic/math-academy-id 2740} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1c568d-36bc-51c0-84f0-502d1edec67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263134} {:answer/id #uuid "15c6b34c-c545-5d3c-acb9-3150c86a0408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263204} {:answer/id #uuid "6991653b-499f-5aa9-839d-82c296e784eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263907} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b70ef3-acdc-5304-9dab-af64de3228a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264404} {:answer/id #uuid "f636dd7a-29d2-590e-895b-f51e6bc5bb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263907} :answer-field/id #uuid "becc03c6-febe-52bf-9a33-abc159de7662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "edcd3710-0bbf-55f6-957d-428926b6e84b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129942" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3bf462e0-cd35-5139-b4b8-076ca61fdfbb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67952861-f148-5199-901d-802ad7eb404f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380279} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8be863-428e-5b87-b241-96a67407c8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380280} {:answer/id #uuid "107ff186-6c17-5ea7-adea-7b445653f698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380281} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b5b7d-1fb2-5652-86d6-70a0293db99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380282} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b43670-c885-5264-801c-5f50ae728f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380279} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6acf30-551e-5b2f-95cc-84791c4dfae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e072db4c-ae23-5874-8e05-5e0e80f81647" :question/math-academy-id "q-90912" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=2w\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=1\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "65ac6d18-371e-5c38-b024-fb1899c0fd0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426293}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf2cf10-889a-5e2c-a4c9-c7d6aadbce53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354772} {:answer/id #uuid "aa16421b-994d-5da6-863d-6ea3a79896bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356170} {:answer/id #uuid "c14821fb-4141-5115-9012-5b4f4293ea36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356356} {:answer/id #uuid "e9843801-abf9-5cf7-a76d-830f177eef7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356706} {:answer/id #uuid "eba3f05e-2836-5879-ae2f-d7c77543ae46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356170} :answer-field/id #uuid "02d6a845-e776-58ed-a2a7-d5b5b0fd2a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25f23664-d443-5442-98c5-f5ec1e9dae76" :question/math-academy-id "q-90763" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx{w}^{′}+6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-5\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99f0162a-1b3d-5a3f-8847-cd9b380d1ed2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426294}}] :activity/title "General Solutions of Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426292}]}
